  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1213 AM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nSunday and early next week remain a mess with two northern \nupper troughs possible along the the upper low over the Western \nUnited States potentially moving near the area. For now, it is \nlikely that we will have westerly/southwesterly flow with all \nthese features that should bring some mid to high level moisture\ninto the area. The increased cloud cover and influence of the \nupper troughs trying to push the surface low east and allow for \ncold air advection should lead to cooler temperatures for that \nstart of the week. For now, the forecast is that temperatures \nlower each day by about 5 degrees with highs forecast to be in \nthe mid 60s be mid-week. With the uncertainty in how strong each\nfeature will be and when it will advance, some ensemble \nsolutions suggest much colder air would be possible. Even so, \nthe chance for freezing temperatures is only around 5-10%. This \nhas also severely lower the snow chances hinted at by prior runs\nin guidance. What hasn't lowered is the chance for \nprecipitation with the influx of mid and upper level moisture. \nWhile unlikely to be widespread or lead to decent accumulations \ndue to dry near surface air, Mon-Wed has a 30-50% chance of rain\neach day.",
